    "lawCode": "Add §2610-a, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4651\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\nrental vehicle reimbursement coverage\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to ensure that consumers utilizing rental\nvehicle reimbursement coverage are able to rent from the rental\nvehicle company of their choice.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nThis bill would:\n\no prohibit insurers providing rental vehicle reimbursement coverage\nfrom requiring an insured to utilize a particular rental vehicle\ncompany, rental vehicle company location or a particular concern;\n\no require insurers to disclose to the insured that the insured has the\nright to utilize any particular rental vehicle company, rental vehicle\ncompany location or a particular concern; and\n\no require insurers writing automobile insurance that includes rental\nvehicle reimbursement coverage to inform the consumer of his or her\nrights granted under this section in a separate written document.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThere have been instances of insurers steering their customers with\nrental vehicle reimbursement coverage to use one particular rental\nvehicle company. Oftentimes, these customers are unaware of the\navailability of other rental vehicle companies, some of which may\noffer less expensive rentals than the company favored by the insurer.\nConsumers should not be forced to use one particular rental vehicle\ncompany or be in any way limited in their ability to utilize the\ncoverage they purchased. When insurers recommend only one rental\nvehicle company without telling the consumer they have the option to\nuse any company, consumers (many of whom are distressed from suffering\nan accident) often hastily agree to rent from the recommended company,\nregardless of the fact that other, possibly less expensive and more\nconvenient, options are available. This bill would ensure that\nconsumers are afforded the opportunity to make an informed decision.\n\nThis bill is based on Insurance Law section 2610, which prohibits\ninsurers providing collision or comprehensive coverage from requiring\nan insured to utilize a particular repair shop, or, in processing any\nsuch claim, recommending or suggesting that the insured utilize a\nparticular repair shop.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n90 days after enactment.\n",
